# The daily protocol drift, filed onto a packet (backlog 19dec171, car 1
# of 8f4e9cc0).
#
# What is live that the tree does not say, and what does the tree say
# that is not live. The comparison already ran on every gate —
# infra/lint/the-live-protocols-are-the-authored-protocols.sh — and was
# thrown away with the gate log; this unit runs the same lint in its
# report mode and files what it found.
#
# WHY THIS HOST. The lint compares the authored bundle in a CHECKOUT
# against the live registry, so it needs both a tree and a route to the
# system of record, and this is the box that has both: boss-gcp-converge
# fetches forge main into /opt/boss every thirty minutes, and the SoR is
# one hop away. The cluster has no checkout (the server cannot compute
# the drift), the tree has no page (it cannot serve it) — which is why
# this is a host timer beside boss-codebase-metrics and not a CronJob.
#
# WHICH TREE. The lint reads the working tree it lives in, so `--repo
# /opt/boss` names the checkout the converge keeps on `main`,
# fast-forwarded to forge main (the converge refuses to run at all unless
# the checkout is clean and on main). The row carries `head` and
# `head_at`, so a measurement taken against a stale checkout says how
# stale it was instead of looking current.
[Unit]
Description=BOSS daily protocol drift (authored bundle vs live workflow registry)
Documentation=file:/opt/boss/infra/protocol-drift.sh
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# One registry read, 47 file parses and a python comparison: about two
# seconds measured. The ceiling is for a contended box, not the work.
TimeoutStartSec=10min
# THE SYSTEM OF RECORD, PINNED INLINE WITH env(1) on every line that
# speaks to it. deploy-services' shared jobs-url.conf drop-in points this
# host's timer fleet at the legacy 127.0.0.1 instance, drop-in env
# outranks unit Environment=, and that instance has never heard of a
# bundle-only kind: the packet would die 400 in ExecStartPre and the row
# would be PATCHed onto an instance nobody reads. Same fix the estate
# observers needed (91ddebfb), and timers-leave-a-packet check 7 makes
# it the rule. Here the pin does double duty: the SAME URL is the
# registry the lint reads, so the drift is filed where it was measured.
#
# ExecStartPre=- because the packet is VISIBILITY, NEVER A PRECONDITION —
# and here the packet is also where the work LANDS, so: if the API is
# unreachable the wrap exits 0 and lets the run proceed, the run then
# cannot read the registry either, refuses with exit 3 and the lint's
# stderr whole, and the failed unit is a state somebody notices. Nothing
# is filed from no comparison.
# AS THE CHECKOUT'S OWNER. The row's `head` is `git rev-parse` over
# /opt/boss, and git refuses a repository owned by somebody else
# ("dubious ownership") — the exit 3 boss-codebase-metrics took on its
# first-ever firing (2026-09-12 05:14Z) with no User=. The siblings that
# read git here say the same. Not `safe.directory` (infra/gate.sh says
# why), and nothing here needs root.
User=david
ExecStartPre=-/usr/bin/env BOSS_JOBS_URL=http://10.20.0.34:7900 /opt/boss/infra/boss-maintenance-wrap.sh maintenance-protocol-drift "Protocol drift"
ExecStart=/usr/bin/env BOSS_JOBS_URL=http://10.20.0.34:7900 /opt/boss/infra/protocol-drift.sh file --repo /opt/boss
ExecStopPost=-/usr/bin/env BOSS_JOBS_URL=http://10.20.0.34:7900 /opt/boss/infra/boss-step.sh maintenance-protocol-drift run
